{
  "id": "Compound::DB00880 - Gene::762",
  "verbalization": "Chlorothiazide, a thiazide diuretic, is linked to Ca4 through multiple pathways that collectively suggest a role in the regulation of calcium homeostasis. Pathway 1 directly connects Chlorothiazide to Ca1, which is involved in carbonate dehydratase activity, a process that may influence calcium transport. Pathway 2 links Chlorothiazide to Metolazone, a compound that binds to Ca4, reinforcing a direct interaction between Chlorothiazide and Ca4. Pathway 3 further supports this by showing that Chlorothiazide is included in thiazide class drugs, which in turn bind to Ca4. The shared ancestry of Chlorothiazide and Metolazone, as well as their inclusion in thiazide class drugs, suggests a broader pharmacological relationship involving calcium regulation. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Chlorothiazide influences Ca4 through its role in diuretic action and calcium-related metabolic processes.",
